# clangd server configuration.
#
# The compilation database is found without any help from this file: clangd walks
# up from the file being edited until it finds compile_commands.json. Generate it
# with `make compile-commands`. It is not tracked: the include paths are absolute,
# so a committed copy would be wrong on every other machine, and a stale database
# is worse than none -- clangd would silently report diagnostics from flags the
# build does not use.
#
# Nothing else is set here on purpose. Warnings come from the -Wall -Wextra
# -Wpedantic -Wshadow already in the compile commands, so a warning appears where
# it is written instead of only in a CI log. Formatting comes from the
# repository's .clang-format, the one `make format` and the CI check read, so an
# edit saved in the editor and one formatted by the target agree.

# Match the compiler the build actually uses. Without this clangd assumes clang,
# and the built-ins it then believes in, and the language standard it assumes, are
# not the ones g++ compiles with.
#
# `c++2b` is the same standard as the build's `c++23` (g++ 13 accepts both), but
# it is the spelling every clang from 14 on understands. A `-std` clang rejects is
# not merely ignored: clangd then throws away the whole command line, include paths
# included, and the file reports its own headers as missing.
CompileFlags:
  Compiler: g++
  Add: [-std=c++2b]
